vms-52c1: alpha-dec-vms (binutils) — emit weak/aliased procedure DEFINITIONS.

Distinct fifth gap in the alpha-dec-vms toolchain, and the first that lives in
BINUTILS (gas + bfd), not the GCC port. Root-caused while chasing why
`decc$_malloc64` never surfaces as a DEFINITION in the Alpha DECC$SHR while
`decc$_calloc64`/`_realloc64`/`_free` do.

WHY MALLOC IS DIFFERENT (not weak-vs-strong at the C level):
  * musl's mallocng compiles its real allocator under the name
    `__libc_malloc_impl` (src/malloc/mallocng/glue.h: `#define malloc
    __libc_malloc_impl`), NOT `malloc`.
  * The public `malloc` symbol is therefore provided ONLY by
    `weak_alias(default_malloc, malloc)` in src/malloc/lite_malloc.c — a WEAK
    alias whose target is the *static* `default_malloc`.
  * calloc/realloc/free are each a plain STRONG public definition, so on the
    port they decorate to a strong `decc$_calloc64`/... (nm 'T') and export
    fine. malloc's only public definition is the weak alias.

THE BUG (two halves, both in binutils' vms-alpha back end):

  1. bfd/vms-alpha.c `_bfd_vms_write_egsd`: a symbol is written to the object's
     EGSD only when `(old_flags & BSF_GLOBAL)` or it is undefined.  gas clears
     BSF_GLOBAL when it marks a symbol `.weak` (S_SET_WEAK sets BSF_WEAK only),
     so a DEFINED WEAK symbol fails the gate and is silently dropped — even
     though the very same routine has weak-emission logic a few lines down
     (`if (old_flags & BSF_WEAK) new_flags |= EGSY__V_WEAK;`) that is
     unreachable for a weak-only symbol.  So `decc$_malloc64` (weak) is never
     emitted into lite_malloc.o at all — `nm` shows nothing, and mk_decc_shr's
     enumerator cannot export what no object defines.

  2. gas/config/obj-evax.c `evax_frob_symbol`: an equated function alias (from
     __attribute__((alias)) / weak_alias) has no `.pdesc`, so its entry symbol
     (`enbsym`, needed by the EGSD writer to emit the procedure's code
     address) is NULL and must be derived.  The existing code (a) only ran for
     BSF_GLOBAL functions — so weak aliases never reached it — and (b) derived
     enbsym by walking the value expression, which is resolved to O_constant
     by this point (X_add_symbol == NULL), so it failed even for STRONG
     aliases ("no entry symbol for global function 'strongA'", i.e. the object
     did not assemble).

FIX (minimal, internal-consistency; no format invented, no name hard-coded):
  * Writer: treat a DEFINED weak symbol as emittable, and as a definition
    (EGSY__V_DEF + the code-address record), exactly as a global — the weak
    bit is already carried separately.  A weak *undefined* symbol stays a
    reference.  This is the case the writer's own dead weak-flag code always
    intended to cover.
  * frob: accept BSF_WEAK function aliases too, and derive enbsym from the
    `<name>..en` entry equate the GCC port already emits alongside the value
    equate (vms-a0c / patch 0003) via a direct `symbol_find`, instead of the
    broken value-expression walk.  This fixes STRONG aliases (they now
    assemble) and, with the writer half, WEAK aliases (they now export).

Result: `decc$_malloc64` becomes a real weak procedure DEFINITION (nm 'W' ->
mk_decc_shr enumerates it as PROCEDURE), forwarding to the real allocator via
default_malloc -> __libc_malloc_impl exactly as on any musl target; calloc/
realloc/free are unaffected (still strong 'T').

Clean-room (Rule 8): derived from public binutils source + observed as/nm/
objdump behaviour only; no VSI/HPE source consulted.

--- a/bfd/vms-alpha.c
+++ b/bfd/vms-alpha.c
@@ -3677,7 +3677,8 @@
       if (old_flags & BSF_FILE)
 	continue;
 
-      if ((old_flags & BSF_GLOBAL) == 0 && !bfd_is_und_section (symbol->section))
+      if ((old_flags & (BSF_GLOBAL | BSF_WEAK)) == 0
+	  && !bfd_is_und_section (symbol->section))
 	{
 	  /* If the LIB$INITIIALIZE section is present, add a reference to
 	     LIB$INITIALIZE symbol.  FIXME: this should be done explicitely
@@ -3731,7 +3732,8 @@
 	  new_flags |= EGSY__V_NORM;
 	  new_flags |= EGSY__V_REL;
 	}
-      if (old_flags & BSF_GLOBAL)
+      if ((old_flags & BSF_GLOBAL)
+	  || ((old_flags & BSF_WEAK) && !bfd_is_und_section (symbol->section)))
 	{
 	  new_flags |= EGSY__V_DEF;
 	  if (!bfd_is_abs_section (symbol->section))
@@ -3739,7 +3741,8 @@
 	}
       _bfd_vms_output_short (recwr, new_flags);
 
-      if (old_flags & BSF_GLOBAL)
+      if ((old_flags & BSF_GLOBAL)
+	  || ((old_flags & BSF_WEAK) && !bfd_is_und_section (symbol->section)))
 	{
 	  /* Symbol definition.  */
 	  bfd_vma code_address = 0;
--- a/gas/config/obj-evax.c
+++ b/gas/config/obj-evax.c
@@ -105,26 +105,35 @@
       *punt = 1;
     }
 
-  else if ((symbol->flags & BSF_GLOBAL) && (symbol->flags & BSF_FUNCTION))
+  else if ((symbol->flags & (BSF_GLOBAL | BSF_WEAK))
+	   && (symbol->flags & BSF_FUNCTION))
     {
       struct evax_private_udata_struct *udata
 	= (struct evax_private_udata_struct *)symbol->udata.p;
 
-      /* Fix up equates of function definitions.  */
-      while (udata->enbsym == NULL)
+      /* Fix up equates of function definitions (__attribute__((alias)) and
+	 weak_alias).  A real definition gets its entry symbol wired at .pdesc
+	 time; an alias has no .pdesc, so derive its entry from the
+	 "<name>..en" entry equate the compiler emits alongside the value
+	 equate (vms-a0c).  The old code walked the value expression, but
+	 equates are resolved to O_constant here (X_add_symbol == NULL) so the
+	 walk failed; and weak aliases, being BSF_WEAK not BSF_GLOBAL, never
+	 reached this branch at all.  */
+      if (udata->enbsym == NULL)
 	{
-	  /* ??? Equates have been resolved at this point so their
-	     expression is O_constant; but they previously were
-	     O_symbol and we hope the equated symbol is still there.  */
-	  sym = symbol_get_value_expression (sym)->X_add_symbol;
-	  if (sym == NULL)
-            {
-              as_bad (_("no entry symbol for global function '%s'"), symname);
-              return;
-            }
-	  symbol = symbol_get_bfdsym (sym);
-	  udata->enbsym
-	    = ((struct evax_private_udata_struct *)symbol->udata.p)->enbsym;
+	  char *ensymname = XNEWVEC (char, symlen + 5);
+	  symbolS *ensym;
+
+	  memcpy (ensymname, symname, symlen);
+	  memcpy (ensymname + symlen, "..en", 5);
+	  ensym = symbol_find (ensymname);
+	  free (ensymname);
+	  if (ensym == NULL)
+	    {
+	      as_bad (_("no entry symbol for global function '%s'"), symname);
+	      return;
+	    }
+	  udata->enbsym = symbol_get_bfdsym (ensym);
 	}
     }
 }
